 The number of infected people $I$ grows with time $t$ according to the differential equation \begin{align*}
\frac{dI}{dt} &= r I \left(\frac{5000 - I}{5000}\right) \\
&= \frac{5}{100} I \left(5000 - I\right)
\end{align*} where $r$ is the rate of spread. This is a logistic differential equation and can be solved by separating variables to get \begin{align*}
\frac{dI}{I \left(5000 - I\right)} &= \frac{r}{5} dt
\end{align*} Integrating both sides gives \begin{align*}
\int \frac{dI}{I \left(5000 - I\right)} &= \frac{r}{5} \int dt
\end{align*} or \begin{align*}
\ln \left|I\right| - \ln \left|5000 - I\right| &= \frac{rt}{5} + C
\end{align*} where $C$ is the constant of integration. Taking the exponential of both sides gives \begin{align*}
e^{\ln \left|I\right|} - e^{\ln \left|5000 - I\right|} &= e^{\frac{rt}{5} + C} = e^C e^{\frac{rt}{5}}
\end{align*} Since $e^C$ is just a constant, we can ignore it in the left-hand side and write \begin{align*}
\ln \left|I\right| - \ln \left|5000 - I\right| &= \frac{rt}{5}
\end{align*} We are interested in finding when $80 \%$ of the population is infected, which means $4000$ people are infected. We solve this equation for $t$ to get \begin{align*}
\ln \left(\frac{4000}{5000}\right) &= \frac{rt}{5} \\
\frac{4}{5} \ln \left(\frac{4}{5}\right) &= rt
\end{align*} To find the time $t$ for $80 \%$ of the population to become infected, we must solve for $t$ in the equation $4000 = 5000 e^{\frac{rt}{5}}$. This gives us \begin{align*}
4000 &= 5000 e^{\frac{rt}{5}} \\
e^{\frac{rt}{5}} &= \frac{4}{5} \\
\frac{rt}{5} &= \ln \frac{4}{5} \\
t &= 5 \ln \frac{4}{5}
\end{align*} Finally, we convert the natural logarithm to common logarithm to get \begin{align*}
t &= 5 \ln \left(1.6\right) \\
t &= 5 \times 0.731 \\
t &= 3.655
\end{align*} Therefore, it takes approximately $3.66$ days for $80 \%$ of the population to become infected. The answer is (H).
